course: dictionary
lesson: 3409
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «Explosion» is a good example. Its main meaning is a sudden loud bursting with great force, heat and noise, like a bomb or a gas going off.
explain: Read this: «A petrol or diesel car is moved by many tiny explosions: fuel burns inside small cylinders, pushes pistons down, and the pistons turn a shaft that turns the wheels.» Here explosion means a very fast burst of burning inside the engine. Ask yourself: what would fit here? A quick, forceful burst.
explain: Now look at the clue words. «A population explosion» means a sudden big increase in numbers, not a bang at all. «An explosion of laughter» means a sudden burst of feeling. Swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Read the clue «An electric car has no engine burning fuel... so there are no explosions, no exhaust and hardly any noise.» The words no exhaust and hardly any noise tell you that the explosions in a petrol car are loud and forceful, so the meaning is the bang kind.
fact: Explosion is a noun, and its plural is explosions.
fact: «Explosion» means a sudden loud bursting of something with great force, heat and noise.
fact: In science, an explosion can be a very fast reaction that gives out heat and gas.
fact: «A population explosion» means a sudden big increase in numbers.
fact: «An explosion of laughter» means a sudden burst of feeling.
exercise: «Fuel burns inside small cylinders, causing many tiny explosions.» What does explosion mean here? | A sudden burst of laughter | A slow melting | *A very fast burst of burning with force and noise | A big increase in numbers
exercise: «There was a population explosion in the city over ten years.» What does explosion mean here? | *A sudden big increase in numbers | A loud bang with flames | A sudden stop | A burst of anger
exercise: Which phrase shows a sudden burst of feeling? | a population explosion | *an explosion of laughter | a gas explosion | a loud explosion
test: Which sentence uses explosion to mean a sudden big increase? | *The explosion of new houses made the town twice as big in ten years. | The car had many tiny explosions. | The fireworks made a loud explosion. | The gas explosion was heard across town.
test: «Gas leaks and fireworks can cause explosions.» What does explosions mean here? | A sudden big increase in numbers | Slow, gentle melting | *Loud, forceful bursts with heat and noise | Sudden bursts of feeling
exam: Hannah says explosion always means a bang. Is she right? | *No, it can also mean a sudden big increase or a sudden burst of feeling | No, it only means a slow reaction | Yes, but only in films | Yes, it always means a bang
